<!--{"title": "Keeping secrets out of Terraform state: a 2026 checklist", "description": "Terraform state has long held plaintext secrets. Ephemeral values and write-only arguments now keep them out. A checklist to find exposure, migrate safely and harden state.", "h1": "Keeping secrets out of Terraform state: a checklist for platform teams", "kicker": "Cloud · Terraform · Security", "lede": "Most organisations using Terraform have database passwords, API keys or private keys sitting in state files, often in plaintext. For years that was simply how Terraform worked. Since versions 1.10 and 1.11 it doesn't have to be, but the fix needs deliberate migration.", "date": "2026-10-28", "datetime": "2026-10-28T00:20:00+04:00", "readtime": "7 min read", "format": "checklist", "tags": ["cloud", "terraform", "security", "platform"], "keywords": ["Terraform secrets in state", "Terraform ephemeral resources", "write-only arguments", "Terraform 1.11", "Terraform sensitive values", "Terraform state security", "infrastructure as code security", "secrets management", "platform engineering", "cloud architect"], "faq": [["Does marking a Terraform value as sensitive keep it out of state?", "No. Marking a value as sensitive redacts it from plan and output display, but the value is still stored in the state file. To keep a secret out of state and plan files, use ephemeral resources and write-only arguments where providers support them."], ["What are Terraform ephemeral resources?", "Introduced in Terraform 1.10, ephemeral resource blocks fetch or generate values, such as secrets, that are available during a run but are not persisted in plan or state files. Previously, reading a secret through a data source stored it in both."], ["What are write-only arguments?", "Added in Terraform 1.11, write-only arguments on managed resources accept ephemeral values and are never stored in plan or state. Because Terraform can't see the stored value, a companion version argument is incremented to trigger an update."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto/", "/technical-due-diligence/", "/ai-architect/"], "citations": [{"title": "Terraform 1.10 improves handling secrets in state with ephemeral values", "url": "https://hashicorp.com/blog/terraform-1-10-improves-handling-secrets-in-state-with-ephemeral-values", "publisher": "HashiCorp", "date": "Nov 2024"}, {"title": "Terraform 1.11 brings ephemeral values to managed resources with write-only arguments", "url": "https://www.hashicorp.com/blog/terraform-1-11-ephemeral-values-managed-resources-write-only-arguments", "publisher": "HashiCorp", "date": "2025"}, {"title": "Manage sensitive data in your configuration", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/v1.17.x/manage-sensitive-data", "publisher": "HashiCorp developer documentation"}, {"title": "Ephemerality in resources", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/v1.11.x/resources/ephemeral", "publisher": "HashiCorp developer documentation"}, {"title": "Write-only arguments (Plugin Framework)", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/plugin/framework/v1.15.x/resources/write-only-arguments", "publisher": "HashiCorp developer documentation"}, {"title": "Terraform 1.10 ephemeral values", "url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2024/11/terraform-1-10-ephemeral-values", "publisher": "InfoQ", "date": "Nov 2024"}]}-->
<p>Ask a platform team where their production database password lives and the answer is usually "in the secrets manager". Ask where else it lives and the honest answer is often "in the Terraform state file, in plaintext, readable by anyone with access to the state bucket". That was the default for years: a secret read through a data source or set on a resource ended up in both plan and state<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What changed</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1.10</td><td>Ephemeral values and resources</td><td>Fetch or generate values during a run without persisting them in plan or state<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>1.11</td><td>Write-only arguments</td><td>Pass ephemeral values to managed resources; never stored in plan or state<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Together they close the gap: a secret can be read from a secrets manager ephemerally and handed to a resource through a write-only argument, without ever landing in state<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. There is one catch. Because Terraform never stores the value, it can't detect when it changes. Write-only arguments are paired with a version argument that you increment to trigger an update, and only the version is stored<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Support also depends on each provider, resource by resource<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>"Sensitive" hides a value from your screen. "Ephemeral" and "write-only" keep it out of the file. Only the second protects you if the state bucket leaks.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Find the exposure</h2>
<ul>
<li>Inventory every state file and who can read it, including CI service accounts and old backends nobody uses.</li>
<li>Search state for secret-bearing attributes: passwords, private keys, tokens, connection strings.</li>
<li>List data sources that read from secrets managers; each one is copying a secret into state today.</li>
<li>Treat any secret that has ever been in state as potentially exposed to everyone with state access.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Upgrade the toolchain</h2>
<ul>
<li>Move to Terraform 1.11 or later so both features are available<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</li>
<li>Upgrade providers and check their documentation for ephemeral resources and write-only arguments on the resources you use.</li>
<li>If you run OpenTofu, check its equivalent features and version support separately; the choice is covered in <a href="/insights/opentofu-or-terraform-2026/">OpenTofu or Terraform</a>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Migrate resource by resource</h2>
<ul>
<li>Replace secret-reading data sources with ephemeral resources where the provider offers them.</li>
<li>Switch resources to write-only arguments for passwords and keys, with a version argument for rotation.</li>
<li><strong>Rotate the secret as part of the migration.</strong> Moving to write-only stops future storage; it doesn't undo past exposure.</li>
<li>Better still, let the platform generate and manage the secret itself, such as a managed database credential stored in the cloud's secrets manager, so Terraform never handles it.</li>
<li>Update shared modules first, using the patterns in <a href="/insights/terraform-multi-cloud-module-design/">Terraform module design</a>, so every consumer benefits.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Harden state anyway</h2>
<ul>
<li>Encrypt state at rest with customer-managed keys and restrict decrypt permissions.</li>
<li>Limit state read access to the pipelines that need it; humans rarely do.</li>
<li>Enable versioning and access logging on the state backend, and alert on unusual reads.</li>
<li>Keep plan files out of CI artefacts and logs, or apply the same controls to them.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>5. Keep it from regressing</h2>
<ul>
<li>Add a policy check in CI that flags secret-bearing attributes set through normal arguments, and secret-reading data sources.</li>
<li>Document the rotation process using version arguments, so it's routine rather than tribal knowledge.</li>
<li>Include state access in access reviews and in your <a href="/insights/soc2-for-ai-startups/">SOC 2</a> evidence; it is often the broadest secret access in the company.</li>
</ul>
</section>

